Title: Scope the rebaseline recipe: which README rows, SPEC citations, and tickets a span actually touches
Created: 2026-09-04
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-04T06:12Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context
`make upstream-rebaseline` (`bench/upstream_catchup.py --rebaseline`) prints a
fixed checklist (`REBASELINE_TOUCHES`): "every standing row" of README.md,
"every premise stamped to a SHA" and "every file:line citation" in SPEC.md,
"a log entry on every open ticket" in tickets/. That blanket phrasing is
truthful but wasteful whenever a span's actual diff is narrow — checked
against upstream v1.13.0..main (2026-09-04, 12 commits: mostly the author's own
pooling-per-model PR #52 plus an unrelated ops/usage-log feature), where the
"counters" slice ticket 0033 is carrying was untouched but the checklist gave
no way to say so short of re-reading the whole page.

`WATCHED` already carries, as code comments, which requirement rows each
watched path backs. That mapping was legible to a human reading the source but
invisible to the script itself.

## Actions
1. `WATCHED_ROWS: dict[str, list[str]]` restates the `WATCHED` comments as
   data; `affected_rows(touched)` splits every row into affected/unaffected
   given the `WATCHED` paths a span actually changed.
2. `CITATION`/`citations_in`/`resolve_path`/`cited_lines`/`citation_drift`
   parse every backtick-fenced `file.ext:N[-M]` citation SPEC.md makes,
   resolve the bare filename against the zoteus mirror, and diff the cited
   lines' content at the reviewed SHA against the new tip — DRIFTED when the
   text differs, unresolved when the path does not resolve uniquely (chiefly
   Zotero-core citations from #6012, which this mirror does not carry) or is
   absent at the baseline.
3. `FILE_REF`/`open_ticket_paths`/`tickets_to_rederive` grep every open
   ticket (`tickets/*.erg`, not `tickets/closed/`) for the bare filenames its
   log entries name, and narrow to the ones whose named file the span
   actually touched.
4. `rebaseline()` now takes `base` (previously computed but not passed) and
   prints all three narrowings inline under the corresponding
   `REBASELINE_TOUCHES` entry.

## Test
`tests/test_upstream_catchup.py` — pure-logic tests for `affected_rows`,
`citations_in`, `tickets_to_rederive` (no network, following the module's own
"the git walking is deliberately not mocked" convention: the git-calling
halves — `touched_watched_paths`, `resolve_path`, `cited_lines`,
`citation_drift`, `open_ticket_paths`'s filesystem read — stay exercised only
by a live run, same as `residue()` already was). `open_ticket_paths` is tested
against the real `tickets/` directory (ticket 0033 names `metrics.ts`, which
is the exact file this session's manual catch-up had just found orthogonal to
it, so the fixture and the question line up).

## Verification
- [x] `python3 -m pytest tests/test_upstream_catchup.py -q` — 28 passed
- [x] `ruff check bench/upstream_catchup.py tests/test_upstream_catchup.py` —
      clean
- [x] Live run against the real mirror (v1.13.0..main, 2026-09-04, 12 commits):
      correctly narrowed README to 15 affected / 4 untouched rows, found one
      genuine SPEC.md drift (`index-manager.ts:800` — `clearStore()` shifted
      to `:801` when a line was inserted above it, hand-verified with `git
      show` at both SHAs), correctly flagged the 7 Zotero-core citations as
      unresolved, and correctly shortlisted 15 open tickets whose named file
      the span touched (0033 among them, naming `metrics.ts` and
      `index-manager.ts`, both true).

## Invariants
- `WATCHED`, `RELEASE`, `ITEM`, `SCHEMA_VERSION`, `PASSAGES_DDL` and the
  existing report path (no `--rebaseline`) are unchanged.
- `REBASELINE_TOUCHES`'s existing entries and reasons are unchanged; the three
  narrowings are additive prints, not replacements of the blanket phrasing —
  the checklist stays the honest "recipe, not a check" it already documents
  itself as.

## Exit criteria
- [x] `affected_rows` scopes README's checklist line to the rows a span's
      `WATCHED` diff can move
- [x] `citation_drift` scopes SPEC.md's checklist line to citations whose
      cited lines actually changed content
- [x] `tickets_to_rederive` scopes tickets/'s checklist line to open tickets
      naming a file the span touched
- [x] `make check-fast` and `ruff check` pass on the new code
